## 2.3  Electrochemical Measurements
All NITRR electrochemical measurements were performed on an electrochemical workstation (Ivium Vertex, Netherlands) in a three-electrode system using the H-type electrolytic cell separated by a Nafion 117 membrane (DuPont) at the room temperature (Fig. S1). Nafion 117 membrane was pretreated in the $5\mathrm{\wt}\%\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ solution at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{\textrm{1h}}$ , rinsed by ultrapure water and then in ultrapure water at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for another $^{\textrm{1h}}$ , rinsed with ultrapure water several times. Typecially, as-prepared sample $(5\mathrm{mg})$ was dispersed in ${900\ \upmu\mathrm{L}}$ of isopropanol (IPA) aqueous solution $(\mathrm{v}/\mathrm{v},1{:}1)$ and $100\upmu\mathrm{L}$ Nafion solution $(5\%)$ , and ultrasonicated for $30\mathrm{min}$ . The homogeneous ink was dropped onto the commercial carbon paper $\mathrm{1cm}\times1\mathrm{cm}$ , mass loading: ca. $0.5~\mathrm{mg~cm}^{-2}.$ ). Pt plate ${\mathrm{(1~cm}}\times1{\mathrm{~cm}}{\mathrm{)}}$ , saturated calomel electrode (SCE) and above carbon paper were used as the counter, reference and working electrode in $0.5{\bf M}$ $\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ and $50\ \mathrm{ppm}\ \mathrm{NaNO}_{3}$ mixed solutions. The potentiostatic test was conducted at constant potentials for $2\mathrm{~h~}$ with a flow rate of Ar for 10 standard cubic centimeters per minute (sccm) and a stirring rate of $400~\mathrm{rpm}$ . Cyclic voltammetry activation was not performed before the experiment, and the $\mathrm{\pH}$ changes before and after the experiment were ignored. The potential reference equation (Eq. 1) was converted into the reversible hydrogen electrode (RHE).
$$
E_{\mathrm{RHE}}=E_{\mathrm{SCE}}+0.241~\mathrm{V}+0.0591\times\mathrm{pH}
$$
## 2.4  Operando XAS Experiment
The NITRR operando XAS experiment was tested in fluorescence mode at a BSRF 4B9A line station and used the home-built H-type cell referring to the NITRR experimental condition. Where the electrolyte was a mixed aqueous solution with Ar-saturated 0.5 M $\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ and $50\mathrm{ppm}\mathrm{NaNO}_{3}$ , and the ink is uniformly dropped on a carbon paper of ${1}\mathrm{cm}\times2\mathrm{cm}$ (with a mass of about $2.5~\mathrm{mg~cm}^{-2}$ ), and the operando XAS experiment is started at a specific voltage without the cyclic voltammetry (CV) activation.
## 3  Results and Discussion
## 3.1  Characterization of MOFs@CuPc‑x Precursors
All of the experimental powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) patterns of the as-synthesized aMIL $@$ CuPc-based precursors (Fig. 1a) agree well with the simulated one of pure aMIL due to the similarity of the strong diffraction peaks between aMIL and CuPc or the lower mass loading of $\operatorname{CuPc}$ . Increased visible-region absorption and a gradual darkening of color with increasing CuPc loading are observable in the UV–vis diffraction spectra (DRS) and corresponding optical photographs (Fig. 1b) of aMIL, aMIL $\mathcal{Q}\mathrm{CuPc}{-}1$ , aMIL@CuPc-2, aMIL@CuPc-3, and CuPc samples, proving the presence of aMIL and CuPc composite. The vibrational peaks of aMIL ${\mathcal{Q}}\mathrm{CuPc}$ -based precursors in Fourier transform infrared (FT-IR) spectra (Fig. 1c) at approximately 1116, 1086, and $725~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ correspond to $\mathrm{C-H}$ in-plane deformation $(\upbeta_{\mathrm{(C-H)}})$ , C–N stretching in pyrrole $(\nu_{(\mathrm{C-N)}})$ , C–N out-ofplane deformation $(\gamma_{\mathrm{(C-N)}})$ except for the characteristic peaks of aMIL [27]. To uncover the link mode between aMIL and
CuPc molecules, X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) was characterized for aMIL, $\mathrm{CuPc}$ , and aMIL ${\ @\mathrm{CuPc}}{-2}$ precursors. The presence of $\mathrm{-NH-}^{+}\left(402.3\:\mathrm{eV}\right)$ and $\mathrm{\bfN-C}$ bonds $(399.4\mathrm{eV})$ among the $\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ group in aMIL is confirmed by typical high-resolution XPS spectra of $\textbf{N}1s$ (Fig. 1d) [26]. In addition, there exist $\mathrm{\DeltaN-C}$ bond $(399.4\mathrm{eV})$ , pyrrolic $\mathrm{{N-Cu}}$ coordination form $(398.6\mathrm{eV})$ , and $\mathrm{N=C}$ bond $(398.2\mathrm{eV})$ for the CuPc sample [28]. Due to the proximate binding energy of $\mathrm{{N-Cu}}$ and $\Nu{=}C$ bonds and higher half-peak breadth of $\bf{N-C}$ bonds given by both aMIL and $\mathrm{CuPc}$ , pyrrolic $\ensuremath{\mathsf{N}}\ensuremath{-}\ensuremath{\mathsf{C u}}$ and $\Nu{=}C$ bonds were joint fitted through deconvolution locating at $398.4\mathrm{eV}.$ . Significantly, typical high-resolution XPS spectra of $\mathrm{Cu}2p$ of aMIL ${\ @\mathrm{CuPc}}{-}2$ slightly shift to the lower binding energy compared with that of $\operatorname{CuPc}$ in view of the electron-donation effect of $\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ group [29], the axial N of $\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ group over aMIL could be coordinated with $\mathrm{CuPc}$ to form $[\mathrm{Ti}_{8}(\mathrm{OH})_{4}\mathrm{O}_{8}(\mathrm{BDC}\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2})_{6-\mathrm{X}}]$ –(BDC-NH)x–(CuPc)x (Fig. 1f). Compared to pure $\mathrm{CuPc}$ , the average coordination number (CN) of $\mathrm{{Cu-N}}$ bond over the aMIL ${\mathcal{Q}}\operatorname{CuPc}{-2}$ increases from 3.72 to 4.20 (Fig. S4, Table S1). Meanwhile, aMIL $@$ CuPc-based precursors retain the tetragonal plate shape of the aMIL matrix (Fig. S5), while CuPc molecules can form nanorod structures due to self-nucleation with higher CuPc loading [30].
## 3.2  Structural Characterization of Cux/NTC Derivatives
The rational design of precursors with variable loading of CuPc affects the dispersity of Cu species among aMIL $@$ CuPc-based derivatives to a certain extent. The PXRD patterns (Fig. S6, Table S2) substantiate the existence of the mixed crystal phase with Anatase and Rutile $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , and the mass ratio $(m_{\mathrm{{Anatase}}}\colon m_{\mathrm{{Rutile}}})$ is 2.34. Besides, a distinct diffraction peak exists at $43.3^{\circ}$ of metallic $\mathtt{C u}$ phase (JCPDS No: 04–0836) just for $\mathrm{Cu}3.2/\mathrm{NTC}$ sample. Brunauer–Emmett–Teller (BET) specific surface area, pore volume, and Raman spectra (Fig. S7, Table S3) of NTC, Cu0.7/NTC, $\mathrm{Cul.}5/\mathrm{NTC}$ , and $\mathrm{Cu}3.2/\mathrm{NTC}$ samples show that $\mathrm{Cul.}5/\mathrm{NTC}$ possess the biggest specific surface area, larger pore volume and the degree of graphitization, which can provide more active sites and enhance the conductivity of supported materials to accelerate the mass transfer of NITRR. The characteristic peaks of the high-resolution XPS
N 1s spectra (Fig. 2a) at 402.2, 400.8, 399.5, and $398.4\mathrm{eV}$ correspond in turn to graphitized N, pyrrole N, pyridine $\ensuremath{\mathbf{N}}\ensuremath{-}\ensuremath{\mathbf{C}}\ensuremath{\mathbf{u}}$ , and un-coordinating pyridine N, revealing the presence of N-doped C and $\mathrm{{Cu-N}}$ species [28]. Additionally, the content of pyrrole $\mathrm{\DeltaN-Cu}$ of $\mathrm{Cu}3.2/\mathrm{NTC}$ sample is lower than $\mathrm{Cu0.7/NTC}$ along with the progressive introduction of CuPc, indicating that $\mathrm{Cu}3.2/\mathrm{NTC}$ contains a small amount of $\mathrm{{Cu-N}}$ species. High-resolution XPS of $\mathrm{Cu}2\mathrm{p}$ spectrum of Cu0.7/NTC (Fig. 2c) has a low signal-to-noise ratio, which can be attributed to the low $\mathtt{C u}$ content. The peaks of $\mathrm{Cul.}5/\mathrm{NTC}$ at 955.0 and $934.7{\mathrm{~eV}}$ can be described as $\mathrm{{Cu}}2p_{1/2}$ and $\mathrm{Cu}2p_{3/2}$ of $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}[31]$ , and the peaks of $\mathrm{Cu}3.2/$ NTC samples at 953.5 and $933.3\ \mathrm{eV}$ are $\mathrm{Cu}2p1/2$ and $\mathtt{C u}$ $2p_{3/2}$ of ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{0}$ or $\mathrm{Cu^{+}}$ [32]. The high-resolution XPS of $\mathtt{C u}$ $2p$ spectra for both Cu1.5/NTC and Cu3.2/NTC display a large half-peak width, indicating mixed valence states of $\mathtt{C u}$ with $0,+1$ , and $+2$ . Further pre-edge and white line peaks of the $\mathbf{\boldsymbol{X}}$ -ray absorption near-edge structure (XANES) spectra at Cu K-edge (Fig. 2d) suggest that the average valence states of $\mathtt{C u}$ successively are $+2\ge\mathrm{Cu0.7/NTC>Cu1.5/}$ $\mathrm{NTC}>\mathrm{Cu3.2}/\mathrm{NTC}>+1$ . As shown in the $k^{3}$ -weighted Fourier-transformed extended $\mathrm{\Delta}\mathrm{X}$ -ray absorption fine structure (FT-EXAFS) and wavelet transform extended X-ray absorption fine-structure (WT-EXAFS) spectra (Fig. 2d, f), $\mathrm{Cu0.7/}$ NTC, $\mathrm{Cul}.5,$ NTC, and $\mathrm{Cu}3.2/\mathrm{NTC}$ have the $\mathrm{{Cu-N}}$ scattering path in the first shell, while $\mathrm{Cul.}5/\mathrm{NTC}$ and $\mathrm{Cu}3.2/\mathrm{NTC}$ sample show the $\mathrm{{Cu-Cu}}$ scattering path in the second shell compared with $\mathtt{C u}$ foil, $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , and $\mathtt{C u O}$ reference samples. The proportions of ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Cu^{+}}$ , and ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{0}$ for $\mathrm{Cul.}5/\mathrm{NTC}$ are $87.9\%$ , $3.3\%$ and $8.8\%$ , the corresponding proportions for $\mathrm{Cu}3.2/\mathrm{NTC}$ are $71.1\%$ , $4.9\%$ and $24.0\%$ in the linear combination fitting (LCF) results of XANES spectra (Figs. 2g and S10), manifesting the generation of metallic $\mathtt{C u}$ with the high temperature-induced effect during pyrolysis. The average CN of $\mathrm{{Cu-N}}$ bond (Figs. 2h and S11, Table S4) for $\mathrm{Cu0.7/NTC}$ , $\mathrm{Cul.}5/\mathrm{NTC}$ , and $\mathrm{Cu}3.2/\mathrm{NTC}$ are 3.99 $(\sim4)$ , $4.02\ (\sim4)$ , and 3.61 $(<4)$ , and the average $C N$ of $\mathrm{{Cu-Cu}}$ bonds for $\mathrm{Cul.}5/\mathrm{NTC}$ and $\mathrm{Cu}3.2/\mathrm{NTC}$ samples are 2.84 $(\sim3)$ and 7.41 $(\sim7)$ based on the proportion of ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{0}$ in XANES LCF.
## 3.3  Morphology of Cux/NTC Derivatives
The dispersion state of $\mathtt{C u}$ species and supported materials among $\mathrm{Cu0.7/NTC}$ , $\mathrm{Cu}1.5/\mathrm{NTC}$ , and $\mathrm{Cu}3.2/\mathrm{NTC}$ were observed by aberration-correction high-angle-annular-dark-field scanning transmission electron microscopy (AC HAADF-STEM) and high-resolution transmission electron microscopy (HRTEM). AC HAADFSTEM image (Fig. 3a–c) of $\mathrm{Cu0.7/NTC}$ reveals that $\mathtt{C u}$ is monodispersed on the NTC and the distance of neighbored $\mathtt{C u}$ atoms is relatively large $(\sim0.72\mathrm{nm})$ . AC HAADF-STEM images of $\mathrm{Cul.}5/\mathrm{NTC}$ (Fig. 3d–f) provide vital evidence for the coexistence form of $\mathrm{{Cu}}$ species with both monodispersity and clusters (small red cycles and big dark yellow circles in Fig. 3f). Further, Fig. $3\mathrm{g}$ (the enlarged image of the blue rectangular area of Fig. 3e) and HRTEM images of $\mathrm{Cul}.5/$ NTC (Fig. S13) uncover the existence of both anatase and rutile $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ on the surface of N-doped C supported materials, which further confirmed with the well-defined elements distribution of C, N, O, Ti, and Cu (Fig. 3h). Furthermore, Cu nanoparticles with exposed (111) crystal plane load on the surface of NTC material for Cu3.2/NTC sample, integrating with the EDX mapping images (Fig. S14).
## 3.4  Electrocatalytic NITRR Performances
The NITRR performances of NTC, Cu0.7/NTC, $\mathtt{C u l.5/}$ NTC, and $\mathrm{Cu}3.2/\mathrm{NTC}$ electrocatalysts were evaluated in $0.5\mathrm{MNa}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ and $50\mathrm{ppmNaNO}_{3}$ electrolytes. As exhibited in the linear sweep voltammetry (LSV) polarization curves (Fig. 4a), the current density $(j)$ of $\mathrm{Cu0.7/NTC}$ , $\mathrm{Cul.}5/\mathrm{NTC}$ , and $\mathrm{Cu}3.2/\mathrm{NTC}$ are higher than that of NTC, and Cu1.5/NTC has the highest current density in the range of 0.2 to $-1.0\mathrm{V}_{:}$ indicating that the introduction of appropriate $\mathrm{Cu}$ species can promote the NITRR process. Compared to the LSV curves of $\mathrm{Cul.}5/\mathrm{NTC}$ before and after adding $50\mathrm{ppm}\mathrm{NaNO}_{3}$ , the potential range from − 0.55 to − 0.95 V versus RHE was selected to quantify the $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ production performance. After the potentiostatic experiment at the potentials of − 0.55, $-0.65,-0.75,-0.85$ , and $-0.95\mathrm{\mathrm{V}}$ for $2\mathrm{h}$ , the products of $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ , ${\mathrm{NO}}_{2}^{-}$ and catalysis substrate ${(\mathrm{NO}_{3}}^{-}\bar{}$ ) were detected via colorimetric methods (Figs. S15–S17). Cu1.5/NTC delivers best $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ yield $\mathrm{(88.2mmolh^{-1}g_{c a t a}^{-1}})$ and lower ${\mathrm{NO}}_{2}^{-}$ yield at $-0.75\mathrm{V}$ , which is superior to NTC, Cu0.7/NTC and $\mathrm{Cu}3.2/$ NTC at this potential (Figs. 4b and S18). Meanwhile, $\mathrm{Cul}.5/$ NTC presents the optimal FE $(94.3\%)$ , $95.0\%$ conversion rate, and $86.6\%$ selectivity of ${\mathrm{NO}}_{3}^{-}$ to $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ (Fig. 4c), which confirms that $\mathrm{Cu}$ is the main active site of NITRR, agreeing well with that the introduction of appropriate $\mathrm{Cu}$ species significantly increased the $A_{\mathrm{ECSA}}$ to enhance the performance of electrocatalytic $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ production (Fig. S19). Meanwhile, $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ yield and FE for $\mathrm{Cul.}5/\mathrm{NTC}$ show no significant decay during the continuous long-term tests at $-0.75\:\mathrm{V}$ (Fig. S20). To ensure the $\mathbf{N}$ source and accurately estimate $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ yield, multi-position in one mode of $^{15}\mathrm{N}$ isotope labeling experiment, indophenol blue spectrophotometric (IBS), and $^1\mathrm{H}$ nuclear magnetic resonance (NMR) methods were engaged by conducting the product from the potentiostatic experiment for $\mathrm{Cul.}5/\mathrm{NTC}$ at $-0.75{\mathrm{~V~}}$ using blank $\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ solution, $\mathrm{Na}^{14}\mathrm{NO}_{3}$ and $\mathrm{Na}^{15}\mathrm{NO}_{3}$ as $\mathbf{N}$ sources (Figs. S21–S23). Based on the prominent characteristic triple and double peaks of $^{14}\mathrm{N}\mathrm{H}_{4}{}^{+}$ and $^{15}\mathrm{N}\mathrm{H}_{4}{}^{+}$ (Figs. S22 and S23) [33], $\mathrm{NH_{4}^{+}}$ yield quantified with $\mathrm{^1H}$ NMR method is very close to the results of IBS method (Fig. 4d). The reliable $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ yield and FE of $\mathrm{Cul.}5/\mathrm{NTC}$ executed in neutral electrolyte are on par with the majority of published $\mathrm{Cu}$ -based electrocatalysts (Table S5).
## 3.5  Restructuration Process Detected with Operando XAS
Operando XAS experiment is frequently employed to accurately record the dynamic structural changes of metal activity sites during electrocatalysis reactions of singleatom catalysts [34, 35]. Operando XAS experiments of Cu0.7/NTC, $\mathrm{Cul}.5/\mathrm{NTC}$ , and $\mathrm{Cu}3.2/\mathrm{NTC}$ were conducted at open circuit potential (OCP), $-0.55,-0.65,-0.75$ $-0.85$ , and $-0.95\mathrm{\DeltaV}$ versus RHE for Cu K-edge using the home-built H-type cell referring to the NITRR experimental condition, and $\mathtt{C u}$ foil, $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , $\mathtt{C u O}$ , $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{acac})_{2}$ , $\operatorname{CuPc}$ are reference samples. There are no obvious differences in the structure and valence state at different potentials in the XANES and 1st derivative of XANES spectra for $\mathrm{Cu0.7/NTC}$ electrocatalyst (Fig. 5a, d). The characteristic peaks in the XANES spectra of $\mathrm{Cul.}5/\mathrm{NTC}$ (Fig. 5b) at $8988.7\mathrm{eV}$ are attributed to the electron transition from ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}~1s$ to $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}4p$ , while the peaks of − 0.85 and $-0.95{\mathrm{~V~}}$ located at ${\sim}8982\ \mathrm{eV}$ originate the electron transition from $\mathtt{C u}^{+}\mathtt{i}\ s$ to $\mathrm{Cu^{+}}4p_{\mathrm{y}}$ [36], which indicate that $\mathrm{Cul}.5/\mathrm{NTC}$ electrocatalyst begins undergoing the obvious transition from ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ to $\mathrm{Cu^{+}}$ at $-0.85\mathrm{V}.$ The 1st derivative of XANES spectra (Fig. 5e) at the potentials of − 0.85 and $-0.95\mathrm{\DeltaV}$ have a big half-peak width at \~ 8979 and $8980\mathrm{eV}$ [37], revealing that $\mathrm{Cul.}5/\mathrm{NTC}$ has the mixed valence states of ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{0}$ , $\mathrm{Cu^{+}}$ , and ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ at these potentials. The characteristic peaks of $\mathrm{Cu^{+}}$ in the XANES spectra and ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{0}$ in the 1st derivative of XANES spectra for $\mathrm{Cu}3.2/\mathrm{NTC}$ at all the potentials (Fig. 5c, f) become more robust with the negative shift of reaction potential, suggesting that the Cu species among $\mathrm{Cu}3.2/\mathrm{NTC}$ experience the significant reduction process of $\mathrm{Cu^{2+}\to C u^{+}\to C u^{0}}$ .
To understand the valence state and coordination environment of $\mathtt{C u}$ species thoroughly for Cu0.7/NTC, $\mathrm{Cul}.5/$ NTC, and $\mathrm{Cu}3.2/\mathrm{NTC}$ electrocatalysts, we pay more attention to resolving the FT-EXAFS, XANES LCF, and WTEXAFS spectra at the representative potentials of OCP, $-0.75,\ -0.85$ , and $-0.95\mathrm{\mathrm{V}}.$ . The $k^{3}$ -weighted FT-EXAFS fitted spectra together with the fitting results of XANES LCF spectra (Figs. S24 and S25, Table S6) show that $\mathrm{Cu0.7}$ /NTC still keeps the dominant configuration of $\mathrm{Cu-N_{4}}$ at OCP, − 0.75,  − 0.85,  − 0.95  V. However, the partial $\mathrm{Cu^{+}{-}N_{x}}$ generated at the $-0.75{\mathrm{~V~}}$ potential could detach from the anchorage of N-doped C among $\mathrm{Cu-N_{4}}$ and undergo the significant restructuration driven by the potential at $-0.85\mathrm{V}$ to form $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}$ clusters with $\mathrm{Cu-N_{4}}$ $(\mathbf{CuN}_{4}\&\mathbf{Cu}_{2})$ , and then further restructuring to form $\mathrm{CuN}_{4}\&C\mathbf{u}_{6}$ at $-0.95\mathrm{~V~}$ . The weak $\mathrm{{Cu-Cu}}$ bond $(\mathbf{k};8.9\mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{-1})$ ) and apparent $\mathrm{{Cu-Cu}}$ bonds $(\mathrm{k};9.9\mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{-1})$ could be detected in the WT-EXAFS spectra of − 0.85 and − $0.95{\mathrm{~V~}}$ for $\mathrm{Cu0.7/NTC}$ (Fig. S26), except the findable $\mathrm{{Cu-N}}$ bonds at the potentials from OCP t $\mathrm{\Lambda_{0}}-0.75\mathrm{\DeltaV}.$ Discriminatively, the CN of $\mathrm{{Cu-N}}$ bonds at OCP, − 0.75, − 0.85, $-0.95\mathrm{V}$ for $\mathrm{Cu}3.2/\mathrm{NTC}$ are 3.58, 2.89, 1.55, 0.41 in succession, showing that the primary configurations of $\mathrm{cu}$ species at OCP and $-0.75\mathrm{~V~}$ are $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}{-}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ , while ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{0}$ nanoparticles (NPs) exist at $-0.85$ and $-0.95\mathrm{~V~}$ (Figs. S27–S29, Table S7). The restructuration behavior in $\mathrm{Cu}3.2/\mathrm{NTC}$ system can be jointly dependent on the $\mathtt{C u}$ loading amount and reaction potential since ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{0}$ content of $\mathrm{Cu}3.2/\mathrm{NTC}$ grows substantially more than that of $\mathrm{Cu0.7}/$ NTC at the same potentials.
Given the $k^{3}$ -weighted FT-EXAFS fitting spectra integrated with the XANES LCF results of Cu1.5/NTC (Figs.  6a–d, S30–S32, and Table  S8), the CN of $\mathrm{{Cu-N}}$ bonds at $\operatorname{OCP,-0.75,-0.85,-0.95\V}$ are 3.90, 3.87, 3.58, 0.59, and the CN of $\mathrm{{Cu-Cu}}$ bonds are 2.90, 4.12, 7.29, and
8.85, so it is reasonable to assume that $\mathrm{Cul.}5/\mathrm{NTC}$ had $\mathrm{CuN}_{4}\&C\mathbf{u}_{3}$ , $\mathrm{CuN}_{4}\&C\mathbf{u}_{4}$ , $\mathrm{CuN}_{4}\&C\mathbf{u}_{7}$ , and $\mathrm{Cu}_{9}$ structures at the aforementioned potentials (inset of Fig. 6a–d). Intrigued by the stability of restructuring Cu clusters, ex-situ XANES, 1st derivative of XANES, R space, FT-EXAFS fitting spectra, WT-EXAFS, and XANES LCF spectra detected 5 and $48\mathrm{~h~}$ after $-0.95\mathrm{\DeltaV}$ in the air (Figs. 6e–g, S30–S33) clarify the transformation of valence states from metallic $\mathrm{Cu}$ to the oxidation state of $\mathtt{C u}$ , accompanied by the incompletely reversible transformation from $\mathrm{Cu}_{9}$ clusters to $\mathrm{CuN}_{4}\&C\mathbf{u}_{3}$ (initial OCP). Moreover, the bond length in the first shell of exposure in the air for $^{5\mathrm{h}}$ after − 0.95 V (1.96 Å) is marginally bigger than that of exposure in the air for $48\mathrm{h}(1.94\mathring\mathrm{A})$ , which can result from the bond between $\mathrm{Cu}$ and light atoms (N/O) during the oxidation process of metallic $\mathrm{Cu}$ , following by the formation of $\mathrm{[Cu(OH)_{4}]^{2-}}$ intermediate on the wet carbon paper of an alkaline solution. Ultimately, intermediates could be anchored again on the $-\mathrm{N}_{4}\mathrm{C}_{\mathrm{x}}$ sites among the supported materials due to the chelating capacity of ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ [24, 35].
The relationships among the CN, Cu loading, and reaction potential were further investigated using the nonlinear fitting curves of the typical reaction potential versus the $C N(\mathrm{Cu{-}N}$ and $\mathrm{{Cu-Cu}}$ bonds) toward Cu0.7/NTC, Cu1.5/NTC, and $\mathrm{Cu}3.2/\mathrm{NTC}$ electrocatalysts (Fig. 7a, b). Potential intervals of the dominant $\mathrm{Cu-N_{4}}$ configuration in $\mathrm{Cu0.7/NTC}$ , $\mathrm{Cul}.5/$ NTC, and $\mathrm{Cu}3.2/\mathrm{NTC}$ electrocatalysts are OCP to $-0.95\mathrm{V} $ , OCP to − 0.85 V, OCP to $-0.75\:\mathrm{V}$ , while the CN maximum increments of $\mathrm{{Cu-Cu}}$ bonds occur at $-0.95,-0.85,-0.75{\mathrm{V}},$ ， indicating that the potentials of electrochemical restructuration are shifted positively with the increased loading of Cu. Consequently, the restructuration behavior among $\mathrm{cu}$ species in this system co-depends on the $\mathtt{C u}$ loading and reaction potential. Furthermore, the possible mechanisms of aggregation driven by potential and redispersion driven by oxidation integrated with chelation for $\mathrm{Cul.}5/\mathrm{NTC}$ are described in Fig. 7c and further verified with the EDX mapping of $\mathrm{Cul.}5/\mathrm{NTC}$ exposed in the air after − $0.95\mathrm{V}$ (Fig. S34).
